 JSON logs) often preferred over plain text in automation systems?", "options": ["It''s easier to search, filter, and analyze programmatically at scale", "It uses less disk space always", "It''s required by all cloud providers", "It removes the need for timestamps"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is a common reason to separate configuration from code in automation scripts?", "options": ["So behavior can change across environments (dev/prod) without code changes", "To make the script run faster", "To avoid needing version control", "To remove the need for testing"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''observability'' mean for an automated system?", "options": ["The ability to understand a system''s internal state through logs, metrics, and traces", "A UI design principle", "A type of database schema", "A CSS accessibility feature"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why might you use a distributed lock in an automation system running on multiple servers?", "options": ["To prevent two instances from processing the same job simultaneously", "To make jobs run faster in parallel always", "To encrypt job data", "To replace the need for a queue"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is ''exponential backoff''?", "options": ["Increasing the wait time between retries exponentially after each failure", "Retrying immediately every time regardless of failures", "Decreasing wait time after each failure", "A method for compressing data"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''graceful shutdown'' mean for a running automation service?", "options": ["Finishing in-progress work and cleaning up properly before stopping, rather than terminating abruptly", "Immediately killing all processes without warning", "Deleting all logs on exit", "Restarting automatically without logging why"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why is testing automation scripts against a staging/sandbox environment important?", "options": ["To catch bugs before they affect real production data or real users", "It''s required by every programming language", "It makes the script run faster in production", "It removes the need for error handling"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is a common risk of running automation with overly broad permissions?", "options": ["A bug or compromise could cause much wider damage than necessary", "The script will run slower", "It will require more logging", "It has no meaningful risk"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''circuit breaker'' pattern do in automated systems calling external services?", "options": ["Temporarily stops calling a failing service to prevent cascading failures, then retries later", "Physically disconnects the server''s power", "Encrypts all outgoing requests", "Compresses response payloads"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why version-control automation scripts just like application code?", "options": ["To track changes, enable rollback, and collaborate safely", "Version control is only for frontend code", "Scripts don''t need version control if they''re short", "It''s only useful for compiled languages"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}]'::jsonb, 70),
('uiux', '[{"question": "What does UX stand for?", "options": ["User Experience", "User Extension", "Unified Experience", "User Execution"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What is a ''wireframe''?", "options": ["A low-fidelity layout sketch showing structure without visual detail", "A finished, fully styled design", "A type of database diagram", "A JavaScript framework"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of a ''design system''?", "options": ["A shared set of reusable components and standards for consistent design", "A single specific webpage layout", "A backend database schema", "A type of user testing"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What does ''usability testing'' involve?", "options": ["Observing real users interact with a product to find pain points", "Testing server load capacity", "Testing database queries", "Testing code for syntax errors"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What is ''user persona'' in UX design?", "options": ["A fictional representative profile of a target user, based on research", "A real user''s login credentials", "A type of animation", "A CSS class name"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What does ''hi-fi prototype'' mean?", "options": ["A high-fidelity, visually detailed and often interactive design mockup", "A backend database prototype", "A low-detail sketch only", "A finished coded product"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What is ''information architecture''?", "options": ["Organizing and structuring content so users can find things easily", "A database backup strategy", "A type of server architecture", "A CSS grid system"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What does ''accessibility'' (a11y) in design refer to?", "options": ["Designing products usable by people with a wide range of abilities and disabilities", "Making a site load faster", "Reducing hosting costs", "Making a design visually colorful"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What is a ''user flow''?", "options": ["The path a user takes through an app to complete a task", "A type of animation transition", "A database relationship diagram", "A CSS layout technique"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What does ''responsive design'' mean?", "options": ["A design that adapts to different screen sizes and devices", "A design that responds to server errors", "A backend caching strategy", "A design with fast API response times"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What is the difference between UX and UI design?", "options": ["UX focuses on the overall experience/flow; UI focuses on the visual interface elements", "They are identical disciplines", "UI only applies to mobile apps", "UX only applies to backend systems"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why is conducting user research before designing important?", "options": ["It grounds design decisions in real user needs rather than assumptions", "It''s required by law in most countries", "It replaces the need for any prototyping", "It only matters for enterprise software"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is a ''heuristic evaluation'' in UX?", "options": ["An expert review of an interface against established usability principles", "A type of A/B test", "A backend performance test", "A user interview transcript"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does Fitts''s Law suggest about UI design?", "options": ["Larger and closer targets are faster and easier for users to interact with", "Smaller buttons always look more professional", "Users prefer text over icons universally", "Color choice doesn''t affect usability"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of a usability heuristic like ''visibility of system status''?", "options": ["Keeping users informed about what''s happening through timely feedback", "Hiding all system information from users", "Making the system status only visible to admins", "Reducing the number of screens in an app"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why use card sorting as a UX research method?", "options": ["To understand how users naturally categorize and organize information", "To test database query performance", "To choose brand colors", "To measure server response time"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is the difference between qualitative and quantitative UX research?", "options": ["Qualitative explores ''why'' through observation/interviews; quantitative measures ''what/how much'' through numbers", "They produce identical data types", "Quantitative research never involves users", "Qualitative research is always faster"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''progressive disclosure'' mean in interface design?", "options": ["Revealing information/options gradually as needed, to avoid overwhelming users", "Showing all features on the first screen always", "Disabling features for new users permanently", "A type of loading animation"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why is contrast ratio important in accessible design?", "options": ["It ensures text is readable for users with low vision or color blindness", "It affects how fast a page loads", "It''s only relevant for print design", "It determines a design''s file size"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is a ''design token''?", "options": ["A named, reusable value (like a color or spacing unit) used consistently across a design system", "A cryptographic security key", "A type of user permission", "A database column type"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''affordance'' mean in interface design?", "options": ["A visual cue that suggests how an element can be used, like a button looking clickable", "A backend permission setting", "A type of animation curve", "A user''s login token"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why might you run an A/B test on a design change?", "options": ["To compare two versions with real users and measure which performs better against a goal", "To test server uptime", "To check for spelling errors only", "It''s required before any design ships"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is the ''paradox of choice'' relevant to in UX design?", "options": ["Too many options can overwhelm users and reduce satisfaction/decision quality", "Users always prefer more options with no downside", "It only applies to e-commerce checkout pages", "It''s a backend performance issue"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''mobile-first design'' mean?", "options": ["Designing for the smallest/mobile screen first, then progressively enhancing for larger screens", "Only designing for mobile devices and ignoring desktop entirely", "Designing the backend API before any UI", "A CSS framework name"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why is it useful to test prototypes with real users before development?", "options": ["To catch usability issues early, when they''re cheap to fix, before code is written", "Prototyping testing is optional and rarely useful", "It replaces the need for developer testing entirely", "It only matters for large companies"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is ''cognitive load'' in the context of interface design?", "options": ["The amount of mental effort required to use an interface", "The processing load on a web server", "A measure of a page''s file size", "A type of color theory principle"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''consistency'' as a usability heuristic help achieve?", "options": ["Users can apply what they''ve learned in one part of an app to other parts, reducing confusion", "Every screen must look visually identical", "It only matters for enterprise software", "It eliminates the need for onboarding"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why document design decisions and rationale during a redesign?", "options": ["So stakeholders and future designers understand why choices were made, not just what changed", "Documentation is only useful for legal compliance", "It replaces the need for user testing", "It only matters for open-source projects"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of empty states in UI design (e.g. ''no results found'')?", "options": ["Guiding users on what to do next when there''s no content to show, instead of leaving a blank screen", "Hiding errors from the user entirely", "Only relevant for error pages", "A backend caching technique"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why is it important to design for error states and edge cases, not just the ''happy path''?", "options": ["Real users encounter mistakes and unusual situations, and good design handles them gracefully", "Error states are rarely seen so they don''t matter", "Only backend engineers need to think about errors", "It slows down the design process with no benefit"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}]'::jsonb, 70),
