ed to compromise a system", "A type of firewall rule", "A backup file", "A password reset link"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What does ''DDoS'' stand for?", "options": ["Distributed Denial of Service", "Direct Data over Server", "Dual Domain Security", "Delayed Data Storage"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of regularly updating/patching software?", "options": ["Fixing known security vulnerabilities before they can be exploited", "Making the UI look different", "Increasing file size", "Improving font rendering"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "easy"}, {"question": "What is SQL injection and why is it dangerous?", "options": ["Inserting malicious SQL through unsanitized input to manipulate or steal database data", "A way to speed up database queries safely", "A method for backing up a database", "A type of encryption algorithm"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does the OWASP Top 10 represent?", "options": ["A regularly updated list of the most critical web application security risks", "A list of the top 10 programming languages", "A ranking of the fastest web frameworks", "A certification exam"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)?", "options": ["Injecting malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users", "A way to share CSS between sites", "A method for cross-origin API calls", "A type of database join"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is the principle of ''least privilege''?", "options": ["Granting users/systems only the minimum access needed to perform their function", "Giving all users admin rights for convenience", "A password complexity rule", "A network speed optimization technique"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''CSRF'' (Cross-Site Request Forgery) exploit?", "options": ["A user''s authenticated session to perform unwanted actions on their behalf without consent", "A weak password only", "A slow server response", "A missing HTTPS certificate"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why should passwords be hashed and salted before storage, not just hashed?", "options": ["Salting prevents attackers from using precomputed rainbow tables against many accounts at once", "Salting makes passwords shorter", "Hashing alone is illegal to use", "Salting speeds up login checks"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is a ''zero-day'' vulnerability?", "options": ["A vulnerability unknown to the vendor and unpatched, actively exploitable", "A vulnerability discovered exactly at midnight", "A vulnerability that self-repairs", "A vulnerability only affecting old software"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of penetration testing?", "options": ["Simulating real attacks on a system to find exploitable weaknesses before attackers do", "Testing a website''s visual design", "Testing database backup speed", "Testing customer support response time"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''defense in depth'' mean as a security strategy?", "options": ["Layering multiple independent security controls so no single failure compromises the whole system", "Relying on one very strong firewall only", "Hiring more security staff instead of using tools", "Using the same password everywhere for consistency"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is a man-in-the-middle (MITM) attack?", "options": ["An attacker secretly intercepts and possibly alters communication between two parties", "A type of malware that deletes files", "An attack that only targets databases", "A vulnerability in CSS rendering"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why is input validation important on both client and server side?", "options": ["Client-side validation improves UX, but server-side validation is essential since client checks can be bypassed", "Client-side validation alone is always sufficient", "Server-side validation is optional if HTTPS is used", "Input validation only matters for file uploads"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''session hijacking'' involve?", "options": ["An attacker stealing or guessing a valid session token to impersonate a logged-in user", "Physically stealing a server", "Sending spam emails", "Blocking a website with too much traffic"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of Content Security Policy (CSP) headers?", "options": ["Restricting which sources of content (scripts, styles, etc.) a browser is allowed to load, reducing XSS risk", "Speeding up page load times", "Compressing images automatically", "Enforcing password complexity rules"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''security through obscurity'' mean, and why is it considered weak alone?", "options": ["Relying on secrecy of design/implementation instead of real controls \u2014 it fails once details are discovered", "A strong encryption method used by major banks", "A required compliance framework", "A method for hiding vulnerabilities permanently"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of rate limiting login attempts?", "options": ["Slowing or blocking repeated login attempts to mitigate brute-force attacks", "Making the login form load faster", "Improving password hashing speed", "Reducing database storage size"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''least common mechanism'' mean in secure system design?", "options": ["Minimizing shared resources/mechanisms between users to reduce the risk of one compromising another", "Using the fewest programming languages possible", "Sharing one admin account across a team for simplicity", "Avoiding encryption to reduce complexity"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What is a ''security misconfiguration'' vulnerability?", "options": ["Insecure default settings, unnecessary features enabled, or improperly configured permissions left exposed", "A bug only found in outdated browsers", "A type of malware", "An encryption algorithm flaw only"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why is logging and monitoring important for incident response?", "options": ["It provides visibility to detect, investigate, and respond to security incidents quickly", "Logs are only useful for debugging performance issues", "Monitoring replaces the need for firewalls", "Logging is only required for compliance paperwork"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "What does ''privilege escalation'' mean in an attack?", "options": ["An attacker gains higher-level access than originally granted, often by exploiting a flaw", "A legitimate admin granting themselves more access", "A user requesting password reset", "A type of DDoS attack"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}, {"question": "Why should sensitive data be encrypted both at rest and in transit?", "options": ["To protect it from being read if storage is breached and from interception while traveling over a network", "Encryption at rest alone is always sufficient", "Encryption in transit alone is always sufficient", "Encryption is only needed for payment data"], "correct_index": 0, "difficulty": "hard"}]'::jsonb, 70),
